---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';
import kidData from '../../../../data/kid-summaries.json';

const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
const { subjects, topics } = data;
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.slug === 'subjects/astronomy/foundational/orbits-and-keplers-laws');
const kidSummary = kidData[topic.slug] ?? null;
const subject = subjects.find((s) => s.id === topic.subject);
const seq = topics.filter((t) => t.status !== 'planned');
const idx = seq.indexOf(topic);
const prev = seq[(idx - 1 + seq.length) % seq.length];
const next = seq[(idx + 1) % seq.length];

const orbitCfg = {
  preset: 'orbit',
  params: [
    { name: 'eccentricity', label: 'eccentricity e', min: 0, max: 0.85, step: 0.01, value: 0.5 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'perihelion distance', expr: '1 - eccentricity', unit: 'a' },
    { label: 'aphelion distance', expr: '1 + eccentricity', unit: 'a' },
    { label: 'speed at perihelion vs aphelion', expr: '(1 + eccentricity) / (1 - eccentricity)', unit: '×' },
  ],
  height: 350,
};

const lawsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'orbits are ellipses, star at one focus', note: 'Law 1 — Kepler 1609. Circles are just the special case e = 0.' },
    { math: 'equal areas in equal times', note: 'Law 2 — the line from star to planet sweeps the same area per month anywhere on the orbit. Near the star the arc is short but fast; far away, long but slow.' },
    { math: 'T² ∝ a³', note: 'Law 3 — 1619. Square of the period scales with the cube of the semi-major axis. Bigger orbits take disproportionately longer.' },
    { math: 'F = G·m₁m₂ / r²', note: 'Newton 1687 — all three laws fall out of one inverse-square attraction. Drag e above and watch law 2 hold at every setting.' },
  ],
};

const chartCfg = {
  kind: 'bar',
  xLabels: ['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth', 'Mars', 'Jupiter', 'Saturn', 'Uranus', 'Neptune'],
  series: [{ label: 'T² ÷ a³', values: [1.002, 1.000, 1.000, 0.999, 0.999, 1.001, 0.999, 0.999] }],
  y: { min: 0.9, max: 1.1, label: 'T²/a³ (AU-years)' },
  digits: 3,
  height: 300,
};
---
<Layout title={`${topic.title} — ${subject.name} — Interactive Atlas`} description={topic.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topic-grid">
      <aside class="topic-aside">
        <a class="back" href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>← all of {subject.name}</a>
        <dl>
          <dt>subject</dt><dd><span class="dot" style={`--dot:${subject.ink}; display:inline-block; margin-right:.4rem`}></span>{subject.name}</dd>
          <dt>level</dt><dd>{topic.level}</dd>
          <dt>time</dt><dd>{topic.minutes} min</dd>
          <dt>tags</dt><dd>{topic.tags.slice(0, 3).join(', ')}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
      <article>
        <header class="topic-head">
          <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>{subject.name}</a><span class="sep">/</span>{topic.level}</p>
          <h1>{topic.title}</h1>
          <p class="lede">{topic.summary}</p>
        </header>

        {kidSummary && (
          <aside class="kid-note" aria-label="Summary for young readers">
            <p><strong>For young explorers:</strong> {kidSummary}</p>
          </aside>
        )}
        <div class="topic-body">
          <h2>The idea</h2>
          <p>
            For 1,400 years the official universe ran on circles. Ptolemy's geocentric system
            (c. 150 CE) described the planets with circles riding on circles — epicycles — and
            predicted their positions about as well as naked-eye astronomy could check. Copernicus
            (1543) moved the Sun to the centre and simplified the machinery, but he kept the
            circles, and so inherited their fudge factors. The man who broke the circle was
            Johannes Kepler, and he did it not with a better philosophy but with better
            <em>data</em>: the obsessive naked-eye measurements of Tycho Brahe, accurate to about
            one arcminute — the width of a coin seen from a hundred metres away.
          </p>
          <p>
            Kepler inherited Tycho's notebooks in 1601 and spent years trying to fit Mars to
            circular motion. He failed by eight arcminutes — a discrepancy almost anyone would
            have shrugged off as observational error. Kepler knew Tycho's data was better than
            that. "If I had believed that these eight minutes could be ignored, I would have
            patched up my hypothesis accordingly," he wrote; instead the discrepancy forced him
            to abandon two millennia of circular dogma and accept the ellipse — a shape Greek
            geometers had studied but never found in the sky. The three laws that emerged (1609,
            1619) are still, to extraordinary precision, the shape of every orbit in the Solar
            System.
          </p>
          <p>
            The animation below sweeps a planet around its star. The two shaded wedges were
            traversed in <em>equal times</em>; watch them stay equal in area even as the
            eccentricity climbs — that is Kepler's second law, live.
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 1</span><span>equal areas in equal times — drag e toward 0 for a circle</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="sliderLab" data-config={JSON.stringify(orbitCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>The three laws, honestly stated</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Law 1 — orbits are ellipses.</strong> A planet moves in an ellipse with the
            Sun at one focus (not the centre). An ellipse is the set of points whose distances
            from the two foci sum to a constant — a stretched circle — and its shape is set by
            the eccentricity e: 0 is a perfect circle, and as e grows toward 1 the ellipse
            lengthens into a cigar. Earth's e is a modest 0.017, so our orbit is nearly round;
            the readouts in fig. 1 show how perihelion (closest approach, distance a(1 − e)) and
            aphelion (farthest, a(1 + e)) spread apart as e grows.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Law 2 — equal areas in equal times.</strong> The line from Sun to planet
            sweeps out the same area every month of the year, wherever the planet is. The logic
            is visible in the wedges: near the Sun the planet's arc is short but fast; far away
            it is long but slow, and the two effects cancel exactly. A comet obeys this law
            perfectly, which is why it spends centuries crawling along its aphelion arc and a
            few frantic weeks diving past the Sun. (Newton later revealed the deeper meaning:
            a straight pull toward the Sun conserves angular momentum, and equal areas are that
            conservation made visible.)
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Law 3 — periods and distances are locked together.</strong> The square of a
            planet's orbital period is proportional to the cube of its average distance:
            T² ∝ a³. In units of years and astronomical units (1 AU = Earth's average distance,
            about 150 million km), the law is exact enough to say plainly: T² = a³ for anything
            orbiting the Sun. Bigger orbits take <em>disproportionately</em> longer — double the
            distance and the year grows by 2^1.5 ≈ 2.8 times.
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 2</span><span>from observations to Newton</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="equationStep" data-config={JSON.stringify(lawsCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>Kepler's third law, by the numbers</h2>
          <p>
            The third law is a prediction machine. Mars sits at a = 1.524 AU, so its year should
            be 1.524^1.5 ≈ 1.88 years — and it is. Halley's Comet has a semi-major axis of about
            17.8 AU, so 17.8^1.5 ≈ 75 years between visits — again, matching observation. Run
            the law the other way and it becomes a tool: if you want an orbit that takes 8 years
            (a useful satellite duration), it must sit at a = 8^(2/3) = 4 AU. No other distance
            will do.
          </p>
          <p>
            The chart below does the law's audit: for each planet it plots T² ÷ a³, which should
            equal exactly 1 if the law holds. It holds — from Mercury's scorching 88-day year to
            Neptune's 165-year marathon, the ratio never wanders from 1.00. One constant,
            nine worlds (and every asteroid, comet and dwarf planet besides).
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 3</span><span>the third law audited — T² ÷ a³ for every planet, in AU-years units</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="chart" data-config={JSON.stringify(chartCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>Gravity: Newton closes the loop</h2>
          <p>
            Kepler had the what; Newton supplied the why. In the <em>Principia</em> (1687) he
            proposed that every pair of masses attracts along the line joining them with
            <code>F = G·m₁·m₂ / r²</code> — a force growing with both masses and dying with the
            <em>square</em> of the distance. The inverse square is the key: the same pull that
            drops an apple must also hold the Moon. Newton tested this brilliantly: the Moon is
            about 60 Earth-radii away, so if gravity weakens as 1/r², the Moon's downward
            acceleration should be g/60² = 9.8/3600 ≈ 0.0027 m/s². Measuring the Moon's actual
            centripetal acceleration from its period and distance: 0.0027 m/s². The apple and
            the Moon are the same phenomenon.
          </p>
          <p>
            This also explains what an orbit <em>is</em> — the most counterintuitive idea in the
            chapter. A satellite is not beyond gravity; it is in continuous free fall toward
            Earth, but moving sideways so fast (about 7.7 km/s for low orbit) that the ground
            curves away beneath it exactly as fast as it falls. Newton's own thought experiment
            asks you to fire a cannon from a mountain ever faster: eventually the ball falls
            <em>around</em> the world. Stable orbits need no engine — only sideways velocity and
            the right pull. And the ellipse falls out automatically: Newton proved that an
            inverse-square attraction permits only conic-section orbits — ellipses bound like
            planets, parabolas and hyperbolas for bodies escaping forever, like some comets.
            Kepler's three laws became theorems.
          </p>

          <h2>Perihelion, aphelion, and a trap about seasons</h2>
          <p>
            Because Earth's orbit is an ellipse (e = 0.017), we pass perihelion — closest, about
            147.1 million km — in early January, and aphelion — farthest, about 152.1 million km
            — in early July. The 3% distance change slightly alters orbital speed (law 2), which
            is why the seasons are a few days uneven: summer-between-equinoxes in the northern
            hemisphere lasts about 93.6 days, winter's about 89. But here is the trap the
            syllabus loves: <strong>seasons are not caused by distance</strong>. If they were,
            both hemispheres would have summer together. They do not — the North has winter
            while nearest the Sun. Seasons come from the 23.4° tilt of Earth's axis: tilted
            toward the Sun means higher, longer-heating sunlight. Distance is a rounding error;
            tilt is the story.
          </p>
          <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the Sun sits at the centre of the orbit." It sits
          at a <em>focus</em>, off-centre by an amount set by e — for Earth, about 2.5 million km
          from the ellipse's geometric centre. And "planets orbit the Sun" is itself an
          approximation: strictly, planet and Sun both orbit their common centre of mass — it
          just happens to lie inside the Sun.</p>

          <h2>The laws at work: three worked problems</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Problem 1 — a missing year.</em> An asteroid orbits at a = 4.0 AU. Its period?
    T = a^1.5 = 4^1.5 = 8 years. No telescope needed for the forecast. <em>Problem 2 —
    checking Halley.</em> Halley's comet: perihelion 0.59 AU, aphelion 35.1 AU. The
    semi-major axis is the average: a = (0.59 + 35.1)/2 = 17.85 AU, so T = 17.85^1.5 ≈ 75.4
    years — matching the recorded visits of 1910 and 1986, and forecasting the next around
    2061. Kepler's law, run on a comet nobody had fitted yet in his lifetime.
    <em>Problem 3 — Earth's own speed change.</em> Law 2 implies Earth moves faster in
    January than July. By how much? The ratio of speeds at the two extremes is the inverse
    ratio of distances: v_peri/v_aphe = r_aphe/r_peri = 152.1/147.1 ≈ 1.034. Earth's mean
    orbital speed is 29.8 km/s, so January's perihelion speed is about 30.3 km/s and July's
    about 29.3 km/s — a full kilometre per second of annual breathing, and precisely the
    asymmetry that makes the seasons' unequal lengths.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pattern in all three: the laws turn positions into <em>predictions</em>. That is
    what made them revolutionary in 1609 — astrology had tables; astronomy had laws — and
    it is what makes them the working tools of mission planning today, with Newton's
    gravity supplying the "why" underneath.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond ellipses: escape, capture and visitors</h2>
  <p>
    Kepler's laws describe <em>bound</em> orbits, but Newton's gravity allows open ones too:
    a body moving faster than the local <strong>escape velocity</strong> traces a hyperbola,
    visiting once and never returning. The Solar System's comets split neatly along this
    line — short-period comets like Halley live in bound ellipses; long-period comets fall
    in from the Oort Cloud on near-parabolic paths, some for the first time. In 2017 the
    interstellar object ʻOumuamua passed through on a hyperbolic trajectory so steeply open
    (eccentricity above 1.05) that astronomers could prove it originated around another
    star — the first confirmed visitor from elsewhere, with two more (Borisov 2019, 3I/ATLAS
    2025) following. Space agencies run the same logic in reverse: a spacecraft that fires
    its engines past escape velocity leaves Earth's orbit forever; one that arrives at Mars
    slightly too fast must brake against the atmosphere (aerobraking) to be captured into an
    ellipse. Bound and unbound, capture and escape: the conic sections are not a geometry
    lesson attached to astronomy, they are the complete menu of motion under gravity.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
          <p>
            Kepler's third law has a hidden mass tag. Newton's derivation gives the full form
            T² = 4π²·a³ / (G·(M + m)), and the +m is a gift: watch a moon orbit a planet, measure
            its period and distance, and you have weighed the planet. Jupiter's mass was first
            computed from Io's orbit this way; the same arithmetic, applied to stars wobbling
            with invisible partners, has found thousands of exoplanets and even estimated the
            mass of the black hole at our Galaxy's centre. The law also found a planet: Uranus
            kept drifting ahead of schedule, and in 1846 Le Verrier computed where an unseen
            perturber must be for the numbers to balance — Neptune was found that same night,
            within a degree of the prediction. Pure paper-and-pen discovery from Newton's
            inverse square.
          </p>
          <p>
            The laws are superb, not absolute. Real orbits feel tugs from every other planet,
            so ellipses slowly rotate — and Mercury's perihelion advances 43 arcseconds per
            century <em>more</em> than all the tugs can explain. That residue sat unexplained
            for 60 years until general relativity (1915) accounted for it exactly — one of the
            new theory's first triumphs, and a reminder that Kepler's laws are the limit of
            Newton's gravity, which is itself the limit of Einstein's. Kepler, who spent his
            early career fitting the planetary spacing to nested Platonic solids (a beautiful
            theory, entirely wrong), would probably have appreciated the lesson: keep the
            eight minutes, follow the data, and the universe will hand you laws — even if it
            makes you abandon your own favourite ideas first.
          </p>

          <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kepler, <em>Astronomia Nova</em> (1609) and <em>Harmonices Mundi</em> (1619); Newton, <em>Principia</em> (1687); NASA planetary fact sheet (orbital elements); Cambridge O Level/IGCSE Astronomy syllabus, unit 8.</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="topic-next" aria-label="Lesson navigation">
          <div><span class="tn-label">previous lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${prev.slug}/`}>{prev.title}</a></div>
          <div style="text-align:right"><span class="tn-label">next lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title}</a></div>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
